.TH DIALTEST 1
.SH NAME
dialtest \- probe: can this emu instance dial a real TCP connection at all
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dialtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Dialtest
is a disposable probe, not a real command. It exists to answer one
question before any TLS handshake code gets built on top of an
assumption: does
.IR dial (2)
actually work in this emu instance, end to end, against a real host?
See
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
in the source tree.
.PP
It tries, in order:
.IP 1.
.B dial->dial("tcp!bsky.social!80", nil)
\(em by hostname, which requires
.B /net/cs
(the connection server) to resolve the name.
.IP 2.
.B dial->dial("tcp!<literal IP>!80", nil)
\(em by a literal IP address looked up once outside emu, which
succeeds even when name resolution does not, isolating DNS/cs from
the TCP path itself.
.PP
For whichever dial succeeds, it writes a plaintext
.B GET / HTTP/1.0
request and reads back whatever the server sends, to prove data
actually flows both ways \(em independent of anything TLS-shaped.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/dialtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dial (2)
.SH BUGS
This is scaffolding for one measurement, not the first draft of a
real command: it hardcodes one host and one looked-up-by-hand IP, has
no argument handling, and should be deleted or absorbed once the real
TLS client (a port of 9front's tlshand.c, not a hand-rolled protocol)
lands and needs its own connection-establishment code.
.PP
Confirmed once, live, 2026-09-02: dial by hostname fails here with
"invalid IP address" (emu/port/devip.c) \(em
.B /net/cs
is not resolving names in this emu instance. Dial by literal IP
works: a real TCP connect, a real HTTP/1.0 request written, and a
real response read back. This is a genuine, separate gap (DNS/cs),
not a dial or TCP bug, and is not fixed by this probe or by anything
in the SSL modernization work \(em tracked, not solved, here.
